### What happens when Elasticsearch sees a new string field?

Dynamic mapping is the behavior where, if you index a document containing a field not in the mapping, Elasticsearch infers a type from the value and adds it automatically. For a string value, the result is a multi-field: the field is mapped as `text`, and a sub-field named `keyword` is added.

```bash
# Index a document with a new field "description"
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:9200/transactions/_doc/1" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"description": "International wire transfer to HDFC savings"}'

# Check the auto-generated mapping
curl -X GET "http://localhost:9200/transactions/_mapping"
```

```text
{
  "transactions": {
    "mappings": {
      "properties": {
        "description": {
          "type": "text",
          "fields": {
            "keyword": {
              "type": "keyword",
              "ignore_above": 256
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### How does the text type work?

The text type is for full-text search. When a value is indexed into a text field it passes through an analyzer that tokenizes and normalizes it. This lets a match query find the document when the user searches for any word, regardless of case.

```bash
# Full-text search on the text field
curl -X GET "http://localhost:9200/transactions/_search" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "query": {
      "match": {
        "description": "wire transfer"
      }
    }
  }'
```

```text
# Matches because "International wire transfer..." contains
# the tokens "wire" and "transfer" after analysis
```

### How does the keyword type work?

The keyword type stores the value verbatim and un-analyzed. It is what you use for structured identifiers and categorical data: account IDs, status codes, currency codes.

```bash
# Exact match on the keyword sub-field
curl -X GET "http://localhost:9200/transactions/_search" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "query": {
      "term": {
        "status.keyword": "COMPLETED"
      }
    }
  }'

# Aggregation on the keyword sub-field
curl -X GET "http://localhost:9200/transactions/_search" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "size": 0,
    "aggs": {
      "by_status": {
        "terms": {
          "field": "status.keyword"
        }
      }
    }
  }'
```

```text
{"aggregations":{"by_status":{"buckets":[{"key":"COMPLETED","doc_count":150},{"key":"FAILED","doc_count":12}]}}}
```

### How is text different from keyword?

| Aspect | text | keyword |
|---|---|---|
| Analyzed | Yes (tokenized, lowercased) | No (stored verbatim) |
| Full-text search | Yes | No |
| Exact match | No (tokens, not whole value) | Yes |
| Sorting | Not efficient | Yes |
| Aggregation | Not meaningful | Yes |
| Use case | Descriptions, notes | IDs, status, codes |

### What is date detection and why is it a gotcha?

Dynamic mapping also infers other types. A string that looks like a date can be mapped as a date if date detection is enabled, which is a common surprise.

```bash
# This string looks like a date -- ES may map it as date!
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:9200/accounts/_doc/1" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"referenceCode": "2025-01-15"}'

# Check -- surprise, it is a date!
curl -X GET "http://localhost:9200/accounts/_mapping"
```

```text
"referenceCode": {"type": "date"}
# If the next document has referenceCode: "REF-ABC-123"
# it will FAIL because it cannot parse as a date!
```

```bash
# Disable date detection to prevent surprises
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:9200/accounts" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"mappings":{"date_detection":false}}'
```

### What are the dynamic mapping modes?

Dynamic mapping has modes you can set per index to control how unknown fields are handled.

```bash
# strict mode: reject documents with unmapped fields
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:9200/transactions" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "mappings": {
      "dynamic": "strict",
      "properties": {
        "amount": {"type": "long"},
        "status": {"type": "keyword"},
        "description": {"type": "text"}
      }
    }
  }'

# This will be REJECTED because "newField" is not mapped
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:9200/transactions/_doc/1" \
  -d '{"amount":50000,"status":"COMPLETED","newField":"surprise"}'
```

```text
{"error":{"reason":"mapping set to strict, dynamic introduction of [newField] within [_doc] is not allowed"}}
```

| Mode | Behavior |
|---|---|
| true (default) | Auto-add new fields to mapping |
| runtime | Add as runtime fields (query-time only) |
| false | Store but ignore in queries |
| strict | Reject documents with unknown fields |

### What are common mistakes?

Relying on dynamic mapping for production doubles index size by storing two representations of every string field.

```bash
# WRONG: dynamic mapping for production -- accountId gets text + keyword
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:9200/transactions/_doc/1" \
  -d '{"accountId":"ACC001","status":"COMPLETED"}'
# accountId is text + keyword, but you only ever filter on it

# CORRECT: explicit mapping -- accountId is pure keyword
curl -X PUT "http://localhost:9200/transactions" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "mappings": {
      "dynamic": "strict",
      "properties": {
        "accountId": {"type": "keyword"},
        "status": {"type": "keyword"},
        "description": {"type": "text"},
        "timestamp": {"type": "date"}
      }
    }
  }'
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer defines explicit mappings for production indices. For a banking audit index at National Bank of India, the account ID and status are pure keyword, the description is text, and the timestamp is date. Dynamic is set to strict so unexpected fields are rejected rather than silently and wrongly mapped.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import org.springframework.data.annotation.Id;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.annotations.Document;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.annotations.Field;
import org.springframework.data.elasticsearch.annotations.FieldType;

@Document(indexName = "transactions")
public class TransactionDocument {

    @Id
    private String id;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)  // Exact match only
    private String accountId;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String status;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Text)     // Full-text search
    private String description;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Date)
    private String timestamp;

    // Constructors, getters omitted for brevity
}
```
